window.docContents[67].resolve({"/Types-and-expressions/Binary-operators/#Core-C______--Types-and-expressions--Binary-operators":{"contents":"The arithmetic operators require int on both operands. The evaluator computes the left operand before the right one, the order Core C++ chooses where C++17 fixes none. Division and remainder truncate toward zero, as in C++. A zero divisor and a result outside the range of int fail a premise, so the expression has no derivation and the evaluator gives error.The quotient of  by  is , outside the range, so it has no derivation. C++17 leaves the remainder undefined whenever it leaves the quotient undefined (N4659 §8.6 paragraph 4). The remainder therefore exists only when the quotient is in the range, and the remainder of  by  has no derivation either.A left operand of class type calls a member operator⊕ instead, rule T-OpBin of .In the code, the case binop of Eval.expr evaluates both operands and calls Eval.binop.\n\nEquality compares two int, two bool, two pointers, or nullptr with a pointer or with nullptr, when  or , the test Ty.comparable. A pointer to a derived class thus compares with a pointer to its base, . Two pointers are equal when they are the same location, and nullptr equals only nullptr. Order compares two int, and there is no order on pointers. The result is bool, and the evaluator computes the left operand before the right one. A left operand of class type calls a member operator, as in .\n\nConjunction and disjunction require bool and short circuit. The evaluator computes the second operand only when the first one does not decide the result. A class never overloads them.In the code, the cases binop .and and binop .or of Eval.expr implement the two pairs of rules.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Types and expressions","header":"3.4. Binary operators","id":"/Types-and-expressions/Binary-operators/#Core-C______--Types-and-expressions--Binary-operators"}});